Often, websites that have user accounts utilise an SQL database that contains usernames and passwords, and matches the inputted password to what is in the table.
This can be a major vulnerability when there is no prompt checking. For example, in the OWASP juice shop website, this vulnerability is present.
It works when a prompt such as "' or 1=1 -- -" is submitted instead of an email or username. This works because the SQL query to authenticate the user is 
SELECT * FROM Users WHERE email = '' or 1=1 -- - ' AND password = ''

This will return everything from the Users table, because 1 always equals 1, and the rest of the query was commented out.
